    "case_id": "mxh068",
    "case_summary": "A 16-year-old female presents with a sore throat and breathing difficulties. She developed cough, congestion, runny nose, low-grade fever, and body aches two days ago. Today, her cough worsened and began producing yellow phlegm. She reports shortness of breath, and her mother noticed a “funny sound” with her breathing that started this afternoon. She has had reduced appetite and minimal fluid intake. She is fully vaccinated, including for Covid-19, and SARS-CoV-2 testing is negative.\n\nOn examination, her temperature is 39.0°C, heart rate is 110 beats per minute, respiratory rate is 26 breaths per minute, and oxygen saturation is 90% on room air. She appears tired, anxious, and ill, with increased work of breathing. Examination reveals nasal congestion but no drooling. Audible inspiratory stridor and moderate intercostal retractions are noted, which do not improve with racemic epinephrine. Breath sounds are present bilaterally without wheezing or rales. The remainder of the physical exam is unremarkable.\n\nWhich of the following organisms is most likely responsible for this patient’s condition?\n\nOptions:\nA. Group A Streptococcus\nB. Parainfluenza virus type 1\nC. Haemophilus influenzae type b\nD. Mycoplasma pneumoniae\nE. Staphylococcus aureus\nF. Adenovirus\nG. Influenza A virus\nH. Respiratory syncytial virus\nI. Epstein-Barr virus\nJ. Corynebacterium diphtheriae\n",
